# Wiggum crash incident — 15.1 run killed mid-Phase-12 (self-recursion hazard)

Date: 2026-07-30
Run: `20260728-224714-2699391`  ·  Workdir: `/root/workflow_orchestration`
Spec: `/root/roadmap/15.1/SPECS.md` (Governed Fleet, 14 phases 0–13)

## One-line cause

Not a crash and not a critic reject loop. The **Phase 12 proposer launched a real `[sdd]`
kanban card to satisfy an acceptance criterion; that card's ai-ops director called
`sdd-run.sh → wiggum run` and chose `/root/workflow_orchestration` as its workdir — the SAME
live repo the parent Wiggum run was operating in.** That is Wiggum-inside-Wiggum on one tree
(recursion + workdir pollution). The proposer detected it, tried to `pkill` the nested run, and
the whole thing ended in a hard-kill with orphaned process trees.

## What was being attempted

Phase 12 AC8 (as originally written) required proving "a normal `[sdd]` kanban card behaves
end-to-end." The proposer took this literally and LIVE:

1. Wrote `/tmp/gate12-sdd-run.sh`; seeded an isolated kanban board on `:3011`.
2. Launched the real `/root/kanban/kanban-worker.sh` `[sdd]` lane against it.
3. The `[sdd]` lane runs an **ai-ops director agent** → `sdd-run.sh` → **`wiggum run`**.
4. The director **ignored the proposer's invented `KANBAN_SDD_WORKDIR` env var** (the worker
   does not read it) and selected `-w /root/workflow_orchestration` — the live repo.

## The failure mode

A nested Wiggum orchestrator + proposer began running against the parent run's own workdir.
The parent proposer's log: *"The director chose `/root/workflow_orchestration` as the Wiggum
workdir — the live repo, not my scratch dir ... That risks polluting this repo. Let me stop
it."* Its `pkill` attempt killed the orchestrator but orphaned children.

## Post-kill state found (2026-07-30)

- Orchestrator (`orchestrator.sh`): dead.
- TWO orphan trees reparented to PPID 1:
  - `timeout 1800 … bebop compass` — the Phase 12 proposer (+ nested `claude`/`node` subagents).
  - `/tmp/gate12-sdd-run.sh` → `timeout 900 kanban-worker.sh` → `claude` director (+ subagents),
    still owning live kanban card `abb3fefa` (owner/edited markers stamped that morning).
- Stale lock `/root/workflow_orchestration/.wiggum/lock` held by the dead run.
- Stray artifacts: `/tmp/gate12-sdd-run.sh`, `/root/kanban/ac8-sdd-workdir/.wiggum`.

## How Wiggum killed ITSELF (the self-kill mechanism)

The run was not killed by a human or by the OS — **the proposer killed its own parent run.** To
clean up the nested/isolated processes it had spawned, the proposer ran broad `pkill -f` /
`ps | grep | kill` sweeps whose patterns were wide enough to match the very processes running it.
This happened TWICE, verbatim from the run log:

1. **Phase 10 (first self-hit), run.log:6682** — cleaning up an isolated `:3009` kanban test, the
   proposer ran `pkill -f 'PORT=3009'`. Its own admission in the log:
   > *"The `pkill -f 'PORT=3009'` matched my own shell's command line and killed the script
   > mid-way."*
   The pattern `PORT=3009` appeared in the proposer's OWN command line (it had exported that env),
   so `pkill -f` matched itself. It survived that one (only "mid-way").

2. **Phase 12 (fatal), run.log:8522–8525** — trying to stop the recursive `[sdd]` launch, the
   proposer ran, in sequence:
   ```
   pkill -f 'kanban-worker.sh'; pkill -f '/tmp/gate12-sdd-run.sh'; pkill -f …
   for p in $(ps aux | grep -E 'wiggum/orch|wiggum/prop|wiggum/lib|kanban-worker|gate12-sdd|…') …
   ```
   The alternation `wiggum/orch|wiggum/prop|wiggum/lib` matched the **parent orchestrator and the
   proposer transport of THIS run** — i.e. the process tree executing Phase 12 itself. `pkill -f`
   matches against the full command line, and a Wiggum proposer's command line literally contains
   `/root/wiggum/orchestrator.sh` / `proposer.sh` / `wiggum-lib.sh` paths, so a pattern meant for
   "the runaway nested run" could not distinguish the nested run from the parent. The sweep killed
   the orchestrator, orphaning the child trees we later found.

**So the causal chain of the kill was:** live `[sdd]` card → nested Wiggum on the same workdir →
proposer tries to `pkill` the nested run → the `pkill -f` pattern also matches the PARENT run →
orchestrator dies → children orphan. The recursion created the mess; the **indiscriminate
`pkill -f` is what actually pulled the trigger on the parent.**

The deeper trap: a proposer cannot safely `pkill -f 'wiggum…'` from *inside* a Wiggum run, because
it IS a `wiggum…` process. Only the orchestrator (or `wiggum stop`) can distinguish parent from
child (it knows its own run id / PID); a `grep`-by-pattern from within cannot.

## Recovery performed

1. TERM'd both orphan process groups/trees by explicit PID (root-first). All descendants gone.
2. Removed the stale lock; removed `/tmp/gate12-sdd-run.sh`.
3. Confirmed no orchestrator/proposer/gate12-sdd process remained anywhere.

Phases **0–11 remained APPROVED and git-checkpointed** — no work lost; the run is resume-safe.

## Root-cause classification

- **Primary:** a Wiggum acceptance criterion that instructs the proposer to run a LIVE `[sdd]`
  kanban card creates a recursion hazard, because the `[sdd]` lane launches a director that
  launches Wiggum, and the director selects its own workdir (env hints not honored). Inside a
  phase whose workdir is a real repo, this loops Wiggum onto itself.
- **The actual trigger of the kill (self-kill):** the proposer's own `pkill -f 'wiggum/orch|
  wiggum/prop|…'` cleanup sweep matched the PARENT orchestrator running the current phase, because
  a Wiggum proposer's command line contains those very paths. `pkill -f` cannot tell the runaway
  nested run from the parent run — so "stop the nested run" also stopped THIS run. (First seen
  harmlessly at Phase 10 with `pkill -f 'PORT=3009'` matching the proposer's own shell.)
- **Contributing:** a raw kill (not `wiggum stop`) orphans the proposer's child tree. The
  proposer runs under `timeout … bash -c … bebop compass`, which spawns nested `claude`/`node`
  and, here, a whole `kanban-worker` tree. Clean shutdown is `wiggum stop [--now]`.

## Fix applied

`/root/roadmap/15.1/SPECS.md` Phase 12 AC8 rewritten:
- The regression sweep no longer includes a live `[sdd]` card.
- `[sdd]` routing is now proven **DRY-RUN ONLY** via
  `KANBAN_WORKER_DRY_RUN=1 KANBAN_DRYRUN_CARD_ID=999 KANBAN_DRYRUN_TITLE='[sdd] demo'
  KANBAN_DRYRUN_RC=0 bash /root/kanban/kanban-worker.sh`, asserting the worker log line
  `SDD MODE [999] DRY-RUN: would launch ai-ops director then …sdd-run.sh …`. This exercises the
  full router and proves the card routes to the SDD lane WITHOUT launching a real director or
  Wiggum run. Criterion explicitly forbids creating/processing a live `[sdd]` card in the phase.
- Spec re-lints clean (`wiggum phases … → exit 0`).

## Lessons (generalizable)

1. Never let a Wiggum phase run a LIVE `[sdd]`/`[team]` kanban card — those lanes can launch
   Wiggum and pick their own workdir. Prove routing with `KANBAN_WORKER_DRY_RUN=1`, or assert
   from pre-captured evidence.
2. The kanban `[sdd]` director chooses its own workdir; `KANBAN_SDD_WORKDIR`-style hints are not
   read by the worker.
3. Stop Wiggum with `wiggum stop` (clean) — a raw kill of the orchestrator orphans its child
   tree and can leave collateral (nested agents, kanban workers, a stale lock, `/tmp` scripts).
4. **A proposer must NEVER `pkill -f`/`ps|grep|kill` by a broad pattern from inside a Wiggum run.**
   It is itself a `wiggum…`/`bebop`/`claude` process, so any such pattern can match its own parent
   orchestrator and kill the run (this is exactly what happened). If a phase spawns helper
   processes, track their explicit PIDs and kill only those PIDs — never a name/path pattern that
   the parent run also matches. This belongs in a standing rule for any phase that starts
   background processes.
5. Phases 0–11 surviving proves the gate-checkpoint model is resume-safe after a crash: clear the
   stale lock, then `wiggum resume`.

